.Dd August 9, 2026
.Dt AMUX 1
.Os amux VERSION
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm amux
.Nd terminal session manager
.
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl a
.Op options ...
.Cm name
.
.Nm
.Fl A
.Op options ...
.Cm name
.Cm command Op args ...
.
.Nm
.Fl c
.Op options ...
.Cm name
.Cm command Op args ...
.
.Nm
.Fl n
.Op options ...
.Cm name
.Cm command Op args ...
.
.Nm
.Fl H
.Cm name
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.
.Nm
is a fork of
.Xr abduco 1 .
It
disassociates a given application from its controlling
terminal, thereby providing roughly the same session attach/detach support as
.Xr screen 1 ,
.Xr tmux 1 ,
or
.Xr dtach 1 .
.Pp
A session comprises of an
.Nm
server process which spawns a user
command in its own pseudo terminal
.Pq see Xr pty 7 .
Each session is given a name represented by a unix domain socket
.Pq see Xr unix 7
stored in the local file system.
.Nm
clients can connect to it and their standard input output streams
are relayed to the command supervised by the server.
.Pp
.Nm
operates on the raw I/O byte stream without interpreting any terminal
escape sequences.
As a consequence the terminal state is not preserved across sessions.
If this functionality is desired, it should be provided by another
utility such as
.Xr dvtm 1 .
.
.Ss ACTIONS
.
If no command line arguments are given, all currently active sessions are
listed sorted by their respective creation date.
Lines starting with an asterisk
.Pq *
indicate that at least one client is currently connected.
A plus sign
.Pq +
signals that the command terminated while no client was connected.
Attaching to the session will print its exit status.
The next column shows the PID of the server process, followed by the session
.Ic name .
.Pp
.Nm
provides different actions of which one must be provided.
.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a
Attach to an existing session.
.It Fl A
Try to connect to an existing session, upon failure create said session and attach immediately to it.
.It Fl c
Create a new session and attach immediately to it.
.It Fl n
Create a new session but do not attach to it.
.It Fl H
Write the session's full retained output history to standard output and exit,
without attaching.
Attaching replays only a bounded tail of the history
.Pq see Fl s ;
this is how the rest of it is reached, for example
.Pp
.Dl $ amux -H my-session | less
.Pp
The dump never throttles the supervised application, so an unread pager cannot
stall the session.
If the session outruns the dump, the oldest bytes are skipped rather than
delaying the application.
.El
.
.Ss OPTIONS
.
Additionally the following options can be provided to further tweak
the behavior.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl e Ar detachkey
Set the key to detach.
Defaults to
.Aq Ctrl+\e
which is specified as ^\\ i.e. Ctrl is represented as a caret
.Pq ^ .
.It Fl f
Force creation of session when there is an already terminated session of the same name,
after showing its exit status.
.It Fl l
Attach with the lowest priority, meaning this client will be the last to control the size.
.It Fl p
Pass through content of standard input to the session.
Implies the
.Fl q
and
.Fl l
options.
.It Fl q
Be quiet, do not print informative messages.
.It Fl r
Read-only session, user input is ignored.
.It Fl s Ar replay
How much output history to replay into the terminal when attaching.
The session records all output, including while detached, but pushing more of
it than the terminal can retain only scrolls off the top -- at the cost of
rendering every byte of it first, which on a long session means waiting
minutes before reaching the live prompt.
Bounding the replay makes an attach land on the live tail immediately.
.Ar replay
is one of
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Ar lines
a plain number: replay the trailing
.Ar lines
lines (default 10000)
.It Ar n Ns Cm k , Ar n Ns Cm m
a byte budget instead: the trailing
.Ar n
KiB or MiB
.It Cm full
replay the entire retained history
.It Cm none
replay nothing, go straight to live output
.El
.Pp
Set this to your terminal's own scrollback depth to fill it exactly; no
terminal reports that depth, so it cannot be detected automatically.
A line count is also capped at 1 KiB per requested line, which bounds the cost
of pathologically long lines.
Regardless of this setting the session keeps its full history, reachable with
.Fl H .
.It Fl v
Print version information and exit.
.El
.
.Sh SIGNALS
.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Dv SIGWINCH
Whenever the primary client resizes its terminal the server process will deliver a
.Ev SIGWINCH
signal to the supervised process.
.It Dv SIGUSR1
If for some reason the unix domain socket representing a session is deleted, sending
.Ev SIGUSR1
to the server process will recreate it.
.It Dv SIGTERM
Detaches a client.
.El
.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ev AMUX_CMD
If
.Ic command
is not specified, the environment variable
.Ev $AMUX_CMD
is examined, if it is not set
.Xr dvtm 1
is executed.
.It Ev AMUX_REPLAY
Default value for the
.Fl s
option, overridden by an explicit
.Fl s .
.It Ev AMUX_SESSION
The current session name available to the supervised command.
.It Ev AMUX_SOCKET
The absolute path of the session socket available to the supervised command.
.El
.Pp
For compatibility with
.Xr abduco 1 ,
from which
.Nm
is forked, the corresponding
.Ev ABDUCO_*
names are still honoured when the
.Ev AMUX_*
one is unset, and
.Ev ABDUCO_SESSION
and
.Ev ABDUCO_SOCKET
are exported to the supervised command alongside the
.Ev AMUX_
versions.
.Pp
See the
.Sx FILES
section for environment variables used in determining the location
of unix domain sockets representing sessions.
.Sh FILES
.
All session related information is stored in the following directories (first
to succeed is used):
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Ev $AMUX_SOCKET_DIR/amux
.It
.Ev $HOME/.amux
.It
.Ev $TMPDIR/amux/$USER
.It
.Ev /tmp/amux/$USER
.El
.
.Pp
However, if a given session
.Ic name
represents either a relative or absolute path it is used unmodified.
.
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.
Start a new session (assuming
.Xr dvtm 1
is in
.Ev $PATH )
with
.Pp
.Dl $ amux -c my-session
.Pp
do some work, then detach by pressing
.Aq Ctrl+\e ,
list existing session by running
.Nm
without any arguments and later reattach with
.Pp
.Dl $ amux -a my-session
.Pp
Alternatively, we can also explicitly specify the command to run.
.Pp
.Dl $ amux -c my-session /bin/sh
.Pp
Attach with a
.Aq Ctrl+z
as detach key.
.Pp
.Dl $ amux -e ^z -a my-session
.Pp
Reattach without replaying any history, or with all of it.
.Pp
.Dl $ amux -s none -a my-session
.Dl $ amux -s full -a my-session
.Pp
Search the whole recorded history of a running session.
.Pp
.Dl $ amux -H my-session | grep -n 'error'
.Pp
Send a command to an existing session.
.Pp
.Dl $ echo make | amux -a my-session
.Pp
Or in a slightly more interactive fashion.
.Pp
.Dl $ amux -p my-session
.Dl make
.Dl ^D
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr abduco 1 ,
.Xr dvtm 1 ,
.Xr dtach 1 ,
.Xr tmux 1 ,
.Xr screen 1
.
.Sh AUTHORS
.Nm
is a fork of
.Xr abduco 1
by
.An Marc André Tanner Aq Mt mat@brain-dump.org ,
maintained by
.An Sean Cantrell Aq Mt sean@premiseai.com .
.Pp
It keeps abduco's transparent detach/attach model and adds non-blocking
buffered I/O, a full-session history ring with a bounded replay on attach
.Pq Fl s ,
and a history dump
.Pq Fl H .
